{"id": "7a614ab9-4dfe-44e0-bd38-b4a4c77e2bc3", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T23:19:04.931245", "source": "user", "parent_id": "0def21fa-40ca-4f56-b3d5-684876c6958a", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Inspection and approach sketch accepted. The absence of the Docta repository is consistent with the design-only track (no repo or compute required), and it is correct that you flagged all Docta capability claims as public-documentation assumptions rather than repo-verified facts. The proposed approach is approved as the basis for the final deliverable; please now produce the full experiment design, incorporating the following requirements and clarifications:\\n\\n1. Grounding and verification plan: state explicitly, as a first execution step, an API-verification protocol against the upstream Docta repository (which entry points/configs/README sections to check, and which design parameters would be revised if the tabular path exposes a single fused diagnosis score instead of separable hard-sample/outlier scores). The design should be robust to that fork — specify metric mappings for both cases.\\n2. Operational definition of \\\"rare pattern\\\": formalize it (e.g., feature-space density threshold, subgroup prevalence bound, and label-confusion condition) so the planted ground-truth mask is unambiguous and the four planted pattern types are precisely defined, including prevalence ranges for the rarity sweep (down to <0.5% to probe the HLM sensitivity unknown).\\n3. Data generation spec: concrete generative procedure (cluster/mixture structure, feature types, label mechanism, noise injection rate), sample sizes, number of seeds, and why the chosen scale is CPU-feasible; state the pip-install/dependency step needed in the final run environment.\\n4. Controls and baselines: keep the null/matched clean dataset, rare-but-easy vs rare-but-hard conditions, at least one unsupervised rarity baseline (isolation forest or kNN density) and one label-noise baseline (cleanlab-style), plus seed-stability check. Make explicit which are controls vs comparisons.\\n5. Analysis plan: decision criteria for concluding Docta does or does not surface rare patterns (pre-registered thresholds on recall@k / AP under the null FPR constraint), and what failure modes would be reported rather than hidden.\\n6. Feasibility section: end-to-end runtime estimate, compute profile (CPU-only), and any dependency/version risks.\\n7. Clearly separate fixed requirements from your own suggested extensions (e.g., optional real-dataset case study should remain optional).\\n\\nDo not execute anything; the deliverable is the written design only.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "921cf85e-30da-4489-bff1-f77f6571c9bc", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T23:19:50.902933", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7a614ab9-4dfe-44e0-bd38-b4a4c77e2bc3", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Produce the full design as stage=result. No execution needed. Write comprehensive design covering all 7 points, separating fixed requirements from suggested extensions. Include metric mappings for fused-score fork, formal rare-pattern definition, data gen spec, controls/baselines with control vs comparison labels, pre-registered decision criteria, failure-mode reporting, feasibility, verification protocol.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design (final deliverable, design-only): Identifying rare patterns in tabular data with Docta\n\n## 0. Status and grounding statement\n\nNo experiment is executed or claimed. The workspace (/workspace) contains only `task.json`; Docta is not installed and network is disabled here, so all Docta capability statements remain public-documentation assumptions until Step 1 below is executed in the final run environment. Everything in §1–§7 is design; §8 separates fixed requirements from my suggested extensions.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Grounding and API-verification protocol (first execution step)\n\nBefore any data generation or diagnosis run, the executor clones the upstream Docta repository (Docta-ai/docta) in the final run environment and performs a **verification pass** with this checklist. Each item is recorded as \"confirmed / contradicted / not found,\" and the checklist item cites the file it was verified against:\n\n**A. README / docs (verify first)**\n1. README's documented diagnosis categories — confirm the tool claims label-error, outlier, duplicate, and hard-sample detection (these underpin the metric mapping below).\n2. README's tabular example or examples directory: find the exact tabular demo (entry script, config file name, sample dataset). Record the exact CLI invocation.\n3. Any documentation of output format: which per-row fields are written (score columns, flags, ranks).\n\n**B. Entry points and configs**\n4. The main entry script (e.g., `run.py` or equivalent) and the config schema under `config/` — confirm the tabular config keys (dataset path, model/encoder spec, seed, output dir).\n5. Whether the tabular path accepts CSV/parquet directly or requires a custom loader class; record the loader's expected column/label convention.\n\n**C. Output/score surface (the critical fork)**\n6. Inspect the diagnosis/report code to determine whether the tabular path emits **separable scores** — at least two of: hard-sample score, outlier/low-density score, label-confusion (latent-label) probability — or a **single fused diagnosis score** per row.\n\n**Fork-handling: metric mappings for both outcomes.**\n\n*Case A — separable scores exist (expected per public docs):* the primary metric for each planted pattern type uses the score mapped by construct:\n- rare-clean (density-rare, label-consistent) → outlier/low-density score;\n- rare-noisy/confusable → label-confusion / hard-sample score;\n- analysis additionally uses each score standalone AND the max (union) of scores, so the mapping choice itself is evaluated rather than assumed. AUROC/AP are computed per score type; the pre-registered decision rule (§5) is applied to the union score, with per-score breakdowns reported.\n\n*Case B — single fused score only:* all pattern types are evaluated against the one fused score; the design's primary claim becomes \"Docta's fused diagnosis score ranks rare-pattern rows highly,\" with secondary descriptive analysis of whether rare-noisy vs rare-clean rows receive different score distributions (Mann–Whitney U on the two planted subgroups). Decision thresholds (§5) apply unchanged to the fused score; the per-score-type AUROC breakdowns from Case A are replaced by (i) fused-score AUROC/AP overall, (ii) subgroup contrast tests, (iii) rank-overlap comparison against baselines. No other design parameters change; the seed-stability check and rarity sweep are identical in both cases.\n\n**D. Environment**\n7. Record Docta's declared dependencies and Python version from `setup.py`/`requirements`/`pyproject.toml`; confirm the pin list for §6's install step matches.\n\nIf item 6 shows the tabular path is absent or nonfunctional in the upstream release, the fallback is explicitly documented: report the finding and run the design on Docta's image pipeline over tabular data rendered as flat \"images\" is NOT attempted (out of scope); instead the deliverable degrades to \"Docta tabular support could not be exercised,\" with the baselines run as a sanity floor. This is a failure report, not a silent redesign.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Operational definition of \"rare pattern\"\n\nLet the dataset be X ∈ R^{n×d} with labels y, generated from K dense \"common\" components C and a set of planted rare components R_j (j = 1..m). Define:\n\n- **Density rarity**: a point x is *density-rare* if its k-NN mean distance (k = 25, on generated features standardized) exceeds the 97.5th percentile of the same statistic over the common components. This is the objective density condition used by the generator and checkable independently of Docta.\n- **Subgroup prevalence bound**: a planted subgroup R_j is *rare* if |R_j|/n ≤ π_max, with π swept in {5%, 2%, 1%, 0.5%, 0.2%} (the two lowest levels probe the HLM/extreme-rarity unknown; each π level gets its own dataset instance).\n- **Label-confusion condition**: a planted subgroup is *confusable* if its class posterior under the generating mixture is ≥ 0.2 for a class different from its assigned label (i.e., an off-manifold or boundary-overlapping region where the Bayes-optimal label is ambiguous or contradicting).\n\n**Planted ground-truth mask**: a row is in the ground-truth rare-pattern set R* iff it belongs to a planted subgroup satisfying the prevalence bound, and every planted subgroup additionally satisfies the density-rarity condition by construction (verified post hoc against the k-NN statistic above; rows failing the check are removed and reported).\n\n**The four planted pattern types** (each defined by the three conditions above):\n1. **R1 rare-clean**: isolated cluster in feature space, label = its own cluster's dominant class, no confusion (tests: does Docta flag density-rare but label-consistent rows?).\n2. **R2 rare-noisy**: isolated cluster assigned labels drawn 50% from a neighboring common cluster's class (density-rare + confusable + mislabeled).\n3. **R3 rare-shifted-minority**: minority-class subgroup with covariate mean shifted away from the class centroid (density-rare + minority + mildly confusable at the boundary).\n4. **R4 rare-categorical-combination**: rare joint category combination (e.g., two categorical features whose combination occurs <π_max) with label consistent with one parent category — tests whether Docta flags combinatorial rarity a density metric on one-hot features may miss (and vice versa; this is an explicit hypothesis-discriminating cell).\n\nAdditionally a **non-rare hard subset** (ambiguous-but-common boundary points, ~2%) is planted as a *negative-for-rarity / positive-for-hardness* contrast: Docta should flag it, but it is excluded from R*, so we can measure whether Docta's \"hard sample\" scores conflate hardness with rarity — a key failure mode to report.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Data generation spec\n\n**Generator**: a single Python script (scikit-learn + numpy only) producing, per condition:\n- **Structure**: K = 6 Gaussian components in a 12-dimensional latent space → mapped to observable features: 6 continuous features (Gaussian per component, heterogeneous variances), 4 ordinal/binned features (bin common components coarsely), 2 categorical features (3–4 levels each, correlated with components). All features standardized for continuous columns; a fixed RNG per seed.\n- **Labels**: y = argmax of component→class map (4 classes); label noise: 5% symmetric flip on common rows (realistic baseline noise, distinct from the planted R2 mislabeling).\n- **Rare planting**: per π level, R1/R2/R3 each get n·π rows (split evenly), R4 gets n·π rows; the non-rare-hard subset gets 2%.\n- **Sizes/seeds**: n = 20,000 rows per dataset instance (10% held out as an unlabeled query set not needed by Docta's diagnosis, kept for score-out-of-sample checks); **5 seeds per π level × 5 π levels = 25 dataset instances** + 5 matched-clean null instances (no planted rare groups) = 30 datasets total.\n- **Why CPU-feasible**: Docta's tabular encoder is (assumed) a small MLP; diagnosis over 20k×~12 features is minutes-scale on CPU (embedding pass + confusion estimation are O(n) in sample count with a small model). 30 instances × minutes = well under a few CPU-hours total. Generation is trivial (<1 min per instance).\n\n**Install step for the final run environment** (not executable here — network disabled): `python3 -m venv venv && ./venv/bin/pip install docta scikit-learn pandas numpy matplotlib` (pin exact versions after §1 item 7 records upstream requirements; fallback: `pip install -e .` from the clone). Report actual resolved versions in results.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Controls and baselines (roles made explicit)\n\n| Element | Role |\n|---|---|\n| Matched clean datasets (no planted rare groups, same size/dims/noise) | **Control** — establishes the null score distribution / FPR of every detector |\n| Repeated seeds (5 per condition) | **Control** — stability of rankings |\n| Non-rare-hard planted subset | **Control/contrast** — measures conflation of hardness with rarity |\n| \"Rare-but-easy\" condition (R1 at larger π = 5%) vs \"rare-but-hard\" (R2 at π ≤ 0.5%) | **Manipulated condition pair** — separates detectability drivers |\n| Isolation forest (sklearn, contamination set to each π level) and k-NN mean-distance ranking | **Comparison baselines** (unsupervised rarity) |\n| Cleanlab-style label-error detection (confident-learning on predicted probabilities from a gradient-boosted tree or logistic model) | **Comparison baseline** (label-noise axis; also serves as the \"single-score\" analog to Docta's Case B) |\n\nPer-dataset, every detector emits one ranking of all rows; evaluation masks come from §2. All detectors consume identical standardized features. No detector sees the ground-truth mask.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Analysis plan (pre-registered decision criteria)\n\nFor each dataset instance and detector: compute AUROC, average precision, recall@k and precision@k where k = |R*| (oracle k), and null-FPR-calibrated variants (k chosen so that precision@k on the matched clean dataset stays ≤ 5% flag rate — i.e., report recall at a fixed 1% flag budget). Aggregate over seeds (mean ± range).\n\n**Decision criteria (conclusion \"Docta surfaces rare patterns\" requires all of):**\n1. Union (or fused) Docta score AUROC vs R* ≥ 0.80 pooled, and ≥ 0.70 in every π level except possibly π = 0.2% (extreme-rarity failure is reportable, not disqualifying, if R1-type detection survives at ≥ 5% and 1%).\n2. Recall@1%-flag-budget ≥ 0.5 on R2/R3 (confusable rare groups) at π ≥ 1%.\n3. Null FPR on matched-clean datasets ≤ 5% at the same flag budget.\n4. Docta exceeds the isolation-forest baseline by ≥ 0.05 AUROC on R2/R3 (confusable) types — i.e., demonstrates value beyond pure density ranking; no such requirement on R1 (where density baselines should suffice).\n\n**Failure modes to be reported, never hidden** (each with its dedicated diagnostic): (a) extreme-rarity blindness (π = 0.2% AUROC collapse — report per-π curve); (b) hardness/rarity conflation (high non-rare-hard flag rate relative to R1 flag rate); (c) score instability across seeds (report Kendall τ between seed pairs of the top-1% rows; τ < 0.5 flags instability); (d) Case-B fused-score inability to separate rare-clean from rare-noisy (Mann–Whitney U contrast); (e) API-verification contradictions from §1 (reported verbatim with file citations).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Feasibility\n\n- **Runtime estimate**: data generation ~30 min total; Docta diagnosis ~10–30 min per instance on CPU (small MLP embeddings + HLM estimation at n = 20k) → 30 instances ≈ 5–15 CPU-hours; baselines negligible (<1 hr total). Total: **under one CPU day single-threaded; ~1–2 hours with 224-core affinity trivially parallelized across instances** (one process per dataset instance; no GPU needed).\n- **Compute profile**: CPU-only, no GPU; memory well under 8 GB per instance.\n- **Dependency/version risks**: (i) Docta's pinned torch version vs system Python 3.x — mitigate by recording §1 item 7 and using a fresh venv; (ii) upstream code rot (configs pointing to removed files) — mitigated by the §1 checklist failing loudly; (iii) cleanlab may pull additional deps — it is isolated to the baseline script and can be substituted by a documented confident-learning reimplementation if install fails; (iv) the environment here cannot verify any of this (network disabled) — all feasibility claims above are estimates to be validated in the run environment, and the design treats a runtime blowup at any step as a reportable finding with the affected sweep cell dropped rather than silently truncated.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Summary of what the experiment would demonstrate\n\nIf the decision criteria pass, the supported claim is: \"Docta's tabular diagnosis pipeline ranks planted rare-pattern rows among its top-flagged samples, at usable precision under a 1% flag budget, for planted subgroups down to ~1% prevalence, and adds value over generic density-outlier detection specifically for label-confusable rare patterns.\" Failure criteria yield an equally specific negative claim with the failing cells quantified.\n\n## 8. Fixed requirements vs. suggested extensions\n\n**Fixed requirements (from the task/researcher clarifications, all incorporated above):** design-only deliverable; §1 verification protocol first with both score-surface forks; §2 formal rare-pattern definition and four planted types with sweep to <0.5%; §3 concrete generator spec, sizes, seeds, install step; §4 null control + easy/hard pair + one unsupervised rarity baseline + one label-noise baseline + seed stability, roles labeled; §5 pre-registered thresholds with explicit failure-mode reporting; §6 runtime/compute/dependency-risk section.\n\n**Suggested extensions (optional, not required for the deliverable):** (a) one real public tabular dataset (e.g., a Kaggle/UCI tabular set) with a *weaker* evaluation — top-1% flagged rows manually profiled as a case study rather than AUROC, since ground truth is contestable; (b) a Docta-config sensitivity check (encoder size, seed) if time permits; (c) duplicate-detection cross-check against one of Docta's other documented categories, only to triangulate score semantics. None of these gate the main conclusion.", "evidence": "Design is grounded on two evidence sources, both stated explicitly. (1) Verified environment inspection (previous turn, execute_check): /workspace contains only task.json; no Docta clone, module, or package anywhere on the filesystem (two `find` scans, `pip list`, failed `import docta`); network disabled; /home/actor empty. Hence no repo-verified API facts exist, and the design makes upstream-API verification Step 1 (§1) with an explicit fork-handling protocol. (2) Public-documentation knowledge of Docta (Docta-ai/docta, ICML 2024 \"Docta: A Doctor for Your Data\"): documented diagnosis categories (label errors, outliers, duplicates, hard samples), embedding + hierarchical-label-model/confusion-estimation mechanism, small-encoder tabular path — all carried as labeled assumptions, and §1 items 1–7 are the exact README/config/loader/score-surface checks that confirm or falsify each assumption before execution. The generator design (§3) is CPU-feasible by construction: n=20k rows × ~12 features, small MLP encoder, 30 instances, estimated <1 CPU-day single-threaded, parallelizable trivially across the 224 available cores. Decision criteria (§5) are pre-registered and conditioned on the matched-clean null (FPR ≤ 5%), so conclusions do not depend on post hoc threshold choice.", "assumptions": [], "uncertainties": ["All Docta API specifics (entry point names, tabular config schema, loader conventions, and whether separable hard-sample/outlier/confusion scores exist) remain public-documentation assumptions until the §1 verification pass runs in the final environment; Case-B fused-score metric mappings are specified but their empirical behavior is untested.", "Upstream dependency pins (notably torch compatibility with the target Python version) and upstream code rot could block execution; the design degrades to a documented failure report rather than a silent workaround in that case.", "Detectability at π = 0.2% is deliberately uncertain (it probes the HLM extreme-rarity unknown) and the decision criteria pre-authorize failure at that level without invalidating the overall conclusion.", "Runtime estimates for Docta's diagnosis step are derived from the assumed small-MLP tabular encoder, not measured; the design treats per-step blowups as reportable findings."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b91b6e58a9e1475585276b8abb567009", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b91b6e58a9e1475585276b8abb567009", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# Experiment Design (final deliverable, design-only): Identifying rare patterns in tabular data with Docta\\n\\n## 0. Status and grounding statement\\n\\nNo experiment is executed or claimed. The workspace (/workspace) contains only `task.json`; Docta is not installed and network is disabled here, so all Docta capability statements remain public-documentation assumptions until Step 1 below is executed in the final run environment. Everything in \\u00a71\\u2013\\u00a77 is design; \\u00a78 separates fixed requirements from my suggested extensions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Grounding and API-verification protocol (first execution step)\\n\\nBefore any data generation or diagnosis run, the executor clones the upstream Docta repository (Docta-ai/docta) in the final run environment and performs a **verification pass** with this checklist. Each item is recorded as \\\"confirmed / contradicted / not found,\\\" and the checklist item cites the file it was verified against:\\n\\n**A. README / docs (verify first)**\\n1. README's documented diagnosis categories \\u2014 confirm the tool claims label-error, outlier, duplicate, and hard-sample detection (these underpin the metric mapping below).\\n2. README's tabular example or examples directory: find the exact tabular demo (entry script, config file name, sample dataset). Record the exact CLI invocation.\\n3. Any documentation of output format: which per-row fields are written (score columns, flags, ranks).\\n\\n**B. Entry points and configs**\\n4. The main entry script (e.g., `run.py` or equivalent) and the config schema under `config/` \\u2014 confirm the tabular config keys (dataset path, model/encoder spec, seed, output dir).\\n5. Whether the tabular path accepts CSV/parquet directly or requires a custom loader class; record the loader's expected column/label convention.\\n\\n**C. Output/score surface (the critical fork)**\\n6. Inspect the diagnosis/report code to determine whether the tabular path emits **separable scores** \\u2014 at least two of: hard-sample score, outlier/low-density score, label-confusion (latent-label) probability \\u2014 or a **single fused diagnosis score** per row.\\n\\n**Fork-handling: metric mappings for both outcomes.**\\n\\n*Case A \\u2014 separable scores exist (expected per public docs):* the primary metric for each planted pattern type uses the score mapped by construct:\\n- rare-clean (density-rare, label-consistent) \\u2192 outlier/low-density score;\\n- rare-noisy/confusable \\u2192 label-confusion / hard-sample score;\\n- analysis additionally uses each score standalone AND the max (union) of scores, so the mapping choice itself is evaluated rather than assumed. AUROC/AP are computed per score type; the pre-registered decision rule (\\u00a75) is applied to the union score, with per-score breakdowns reported.\\n\\n*Case B \\u2014 single fused score only:* all pattern types are evaluated against the one fused score; the design's primary claim becomes \\\"Docta's fused diagnosis score ranks rare-pattern rows highly,\\\" with secondary descriptive analysis of whether rare-noisy vs rare-clean rows receive different score distributions (Mann\\u2013Whitney U on the two planted subgroups). Decision thresholds (\\u00a75) apply unchanged to the fused score; the per-score-type AUROC breakdowns from Case A are replaced by (i) fused-score AUROC/AP overall, (ii) subgroup contrast tests, (iii) rank-overlap comparison against baselines. No other design parameters change; the seed-stability check and rarity sweep are identical in both cases.\\n\\n**D. Environment**\\n7. Record Docta's declared dependencies and Python version from `setup.py`/`requirements`/`pyproject.toml`; confirm the pin list for \\u00a76's install step matches.\\n\\nIf item 6 shows the tabular path is absent or nonfunctional in the upstream release, the fallback is explicitly documented: report the finding and run the design on Docta's image pipeline over tabular data rendered as flat \\\"images\\\" is NOT attempted (out of scope); instead the deliverable degrades to \\\"Docta tabular support could not be exercised,\\\" with the baselines run as a sanity floor. This is a failure report, not a silent redesign.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Operational definition of \\\"rare pattern\\\"\\n\\nLet the dataset be X \\u2208 R^{n\\u00d7d} with labels y, generated from K dense \\\"common\\\" components C and a set of planted rare components R_j (j = 1..m). Define:\\n\\n- **Density rarity**: a point x is *density-rare* if its k-NN mean distance (k = 25, on generated features standardized) exceeds the 97.5th percentile of the same statistic over the common components. This is the objective density condition used by the generator and checkable independently of Docta.\\n- **Subgroup prevalence bound**: a planted subgroup R_j is *rare* if |R_j|/n \\u2264 \\u03c0_max, with \\u03c0 swept in {5%, 2%, 1%, 0.5%, 0.2%} (the two lowest levels probe the HLM/extreme-rarity unknown; each \\u03c0 level gets its own dataset instance).\\n- **Label-confusion condition**: a planted subgroup is *confusable* if its class posterior under the generating mixture is \\u2265 0.2 for a class different from its assigned label (i.e., an off-manifold or boundary-overlapping region where the Bayes-optimal label is ambiguous or contradicting).\\n\\n**Planted ground-truth mask**: a row is in the ground-truth rare-pattern set R* iff it belongs to a planted subgroup satisfying the prevalence bound, and every planted subgroup additionally satisfies the density-rarity condition by construction (verified post hoc against the k-NN statistic above; rows failing the check are removed and reported).\\n\\n**The four planted pattern types** (each defined by the three conditions above):\\n1. **R1 rare-clean**: isolated cluster in feature space, label = its own cluster's dominant class, no confusion (tests: does Docta flag density-rare but label-consistent rows?).\\n2. **R2 rare-noisy**: isolated cluster assigned labels drawn 50% from a neighboring common cluster's class (density-rare + confusable + mislabeled).\\n3. **R3 rare-shifted-minority**: minority-class subgroup with covariate mean shifted away from the class centroid (density-rare + minority + mildly confusable at the boundary).\\n4. **R4 rare-categorical-combination**: rare joint category combination (e.g., two categorical features whose combination occurs <\\u03c0_max) with label consistent with one parent category \\u2014 tests whether Docta flags combinatorial rarity a density metric on one-hot features may miss (and vice versa; this is an explicit hypothesis-discriminating cell).\\n\\nAdditionally a **non-rare hard subset** (ambiguous-but-common boundary points, ~2%) is planted as a *negative-for-rarity / positive-for-hardness* contrast: Docta should flag it, but it is excluded from R*, so we can measure whether Docta's \\\"hard sample\\\" scores conflate hardness with rarity \\u2014 a key failure mode to report.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Data generation spec\\n\\n**Generator**: a single Python script (scikit-learn + numpy only) producing, per condition:\\n- **Structure**: K = 6 Gaussian components in a 12-dimensional latent space \\u2192 mapped to observable features: 6 continuous features (Gaussian per component, heterogeneous variances), 4 ordinal/binned features (bin common components coarsely), 2 categorical features (3\\u20134 levels each, correlated with components). All features standardized for continuous columns; a fixed RNG per seed.\\n- **Labels**: y = argmax of component\\u2192class map (4 classes); label noise: 5% symmetric flip on common rows (realistic baseline noise, distinct from the planted R2 mislabeling).\\n- **Rare planting**: per \\u03c0 level, R1/R2/R3 each get n\\u00b7\\u03c0 rows (split evenly), R4 gets n\\u00b7\\u03c0 rows; the non-rare-hard subset gets 2%.\\n- **Sizes/seeds**: n = 20,000 rows per dataset instance (10% held out as an unlabeled query set not needed by Docta's diagnosis, kept for score-out-of-sample checks); **5 seeds per \\u03c0 level \\u00d7 5 \\u03c0 levels = 25 dataset instances** + 5 matched-clean null instances (no planted rare groups) = 30 datasets total.\\n- **Why CPU-feasible**: Docta's tabular encoder is (assumed) a small MLP; diagnosis over 20k\\u00d7~12 features is minutes-scale on CPU (embedding pass + confusion estimation are O(n) in sample count with a small model). 30 instances \\u00d7 minutes = well under a few CPU-hours total. Generation is trivial (<1 min per instance).\\n\\n**Install step for the final run environment** (not executable here \\u2014 network disabled): `python3 -m venv venv && ./venv/bin/pip install docta scikit-learn pandas numpy matplotlib` (pin exact versions after \\u00a71 item 7 records upstream requirements; fallback: `pip install -e .` from the clone). Report actual resolved versions in results.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Controls and baselines (roles made explicit)\\n\\n| Element | Role |\\n|---|---|\\n| Matched clean datasets (no planted rare groups, same size/dims/noise) | **Control** \\u2014 establishes the null score distribution / FPR of every detector |\\n| Repeated seeds (5 per condition) | **Control** \\u2014 stability of rankings |\\n| Non-rare-hard planted subset | **Control/contrast** \\u2014 measures conflation of hardness with rarity |\\n| \\\"Rare-but-easy\\\" condition (R1 at larger \\u03c0 = 5%) vs \\\"rare-but-hard\\\" (R2 at \\u03c0 \\u2264 0.5%) | **Manipulated condition pair** \\u2014 separates detectability drivers |\\n| Isolation forest (sklearn, contamination set to each \\u03c0 level) and k-NN mean-distance ranking | **Comparison baselines** (unsupervised rarity) |\\n| Cleanlab-style label-error detection (confident-learning on predicted probabilities from a gradient-boosted tree or logistic model) | **Comparison baseline** (label-noise axis; also serves as the \\\"single-score\\\" analog to Docta's Case B) |\\n\\nPer-dataset, every detector emits one ranking of all rows; evaluation masks come from \\u00a72. All detectors consume identical standardized features. No detector sees the ground-truth mask.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Analysis plan (pre-registered decision criteria)\\n\\nFor each dataset instance and detector: compute AUROC, average precision, recall@k and precision@k where k = |R*| (oracle k), and null-FPR-calibrated variants (k chosen so that precision@k on the matched clean dataset stays \\u2264 5% flag rate \\u2014 i.e., report recall at a fixed 1% flag budget). Aggregate over seeds (mean \\u00b1 range).\\n\\n**Decision criteria (conclusion \\\"Docta surfaces rare patterns\\\" requires all of):**\\n1. Union (or fused) Docta score AUROC vs R* \\u2265 0.80 pooled, and \\u2265 0.70 in every \\u03c0 level except possibly \\u03c0 = 0.2% (extreme-rarity failure is reportable, not disqualifying, if R1-type detection survives at \\u2265 5% and 1%).\\n2. Recall@1%-flag-budget \\u2265 0.5 on R2/R3 (confusable rare groups) at \\u03c0 \\u2265 1%.\\n3. Null FPR on matched-clean datasets \\u2264 5% at the same flag budget.\\n4. Docta exceeds the isolation-forest baseline by \\u2265 0.05 AUROC on R2/R3 (confusable) types \\u2014 i.e., demonstrates value beyond pure density ranking; no such requirement on R1 (where density baselines should suffice).\\n\\n**Failure modes to be reported, never hidden** (each with its dedicated diagnostic): (a) extreme-rarity blindness (\\u03c0 = 0.2% AUROC collapse \\u2014 report per-\\u03c0 curve); (b) hardness/rarity conflation (high non-rare-hard flag rate relative to R1 flag rate); (c) score instability across seeds (report Kendall \\u03c4 between seed pairs of the top-1% rows; \\u03c4 < 0.5 flags instability); (d) Case-B fused-score inability to separate rare-clean from rare-noisy (Mann\\u2013Whitney U contrast); (e) API-verification contradictions from \\u00a71 (reported verbatim with file citations).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Feasibility\\n\\n- **Runtime estimate**: data generation ~30 min total; Docta diagnosis ~10\\u201330 min per instance on CPU (small MLP embeddings + HLM estimation at n = 20k) \\u2192 30 instances \\u2248 5\\u201315 CPU-hours; baselines negligible (<1 hr total). Total: **under one CPU day single-threaded; ~1\\u20132 hours with 224-core affinity trivially parallelized across instances** (one process per dataset instance; no GPU needed).\\n- **Compute profile**: CPU-only, no GPU; memory well under 8 GB per instance.\\n- **Dependency/version risks**: (i) Docta's pinned torch version vs system Python 3.x \\u2014 mitigate by recording \\u00a71 item 7 and using a fresh venv; (ii) upstream code rot (configs pointing to removed files) \\u2014 mitigated by the \\u00a71 checklist failing loudly; (iii) cleanlab may pull additional deps \\u2014 it is isolated to the baseline script and can be substituted by a documented confident-learning reimplementation if install fails; (iv) the environment here cannot verify any of this (network disabled) \\u2014 all feasibility claims above are estimates to be validated in the run environment, and the design treats a runtime blowup at any step as a reportable finding with the affected sweep cell dropped rather than silently truncated.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Summary of what the experiment would demonstrate\\n\\nIf the decision criteria pass, the supported claim is: \\\"Docta's tabular diagnosis pipeline ranks planted rare-pattern rows among its top-flagged samples, at usable precision under a 1% flag budget, for planted subgroups down to ~1% prevalence, and adds value over generic density-outlier detection specifically for label-confusable rare patterns.\\\" Failure criteria yield an equally specific negative claim with the failing cells quantified.\\n\\n## 8. Fixed requirements vs. suggested extensions\\n\\n**Fixed requirements (from the task/researcher clarifications, all incorporated above):** design-only deliverable; \\u00a71 verification protocol first with both score-surface forks; \\u00a72 formal rare-pattern definition and four planted types with sweep to <0.5%; \\u00a73 concrete generator spec, sizes, seeds, install step; \\u00a74 null control + easy/hard pair + one unsupervised rarity baseline + one label-noise baseline + seed stability, roles labeled; \\u00a75 pre-registered thresholds with explicit failure-mode reporting; \\u00a76 runtime/compute/dependency-risk section.\\n\\n**Suggested extensions (optional, not required for the deliverable):** (a) one real public tabular dataset (e.g., a Kaggle/UCI tabular set) with a *weaker* evaluation \\u2014 top-1% flagged rows manually profiled as a case study rather than AUROC, since ground truth is contestable; (b) a Docta-config sensitivity check (encoder size, seed) if time permits; (c) duplicate-detection cross-check against one of Docta's other documented categories, only to triangulate score semantics. None of these gate the main conclusion.\", \"evidence\": \"Design is grounded on two evidence sources, both stated explicitly. (1) Verified environment inspection (previous turn, execute_check): /workspace contains only task.json; no Docta clone, module, or package anywhere on the filesystem (two `find` scans, `pip list`, failed `import docta`); network disabled; /home/actor empty. Hence no repo-verified API facts exist, and the design makes upstream-API verification Step 1 (\\u00a71) with an explicit fork-handling protocol. (2) Public-documentation knowledge of Docta (Docta-ai/docta, ICML 2024 \\\"Docta: A Doctor for Your Data\\\"): documented diagnosis categories (label errors, outliers, duplicates, hard samples), embedding + hierarchical-label-model/confusion-estimation mechanism, small-encoder tabular path \\u2014 all carried as labeled assumptions, and \\u00a71 items 1\\u20137 are the exact README/config/loader/score-surface checks that confirm or falsify each assumption before execution. The generator design (\\u00a73) is CPU-feasible by construction: n=20k rows \\u00d7 ~12 features, small MLP encoder, 30 instances, estimated <1 CPU-day single-threaded, parallelizable trivially across the 224 available cores. Decision criteria (\\u00a75) are pre-registered and conditioned on the matched-clean null (FPR \\u2264 5%), so conclusions do not depend on post hoc threshold choice.\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"summary\": \"Deliver full rare-pattern Docta experiment design\", \"uncertainties\": [\"All Docta API specifics (entry point names, tabular config schema, loader conventions, and whether separable hard-sample/outlier/confusion scores exist) remain public-documentation assumptions until the \\u00a71 verification pass runs in the final environment; Case-B fused-score metric mappings are specified but their empirical behavior is untested.\", \"Upstream dependency pins (notably torch compatibility with the target Python version) and upstream code rot could block execution; the design degrades to a documented failure report rather than a silent workaround in that case.\", \"Detectability at \\u03c0 = 0.2% is deliberately uncertain (it probes the HLM extreme-rarity unknown) and the decision criteria pre-authorize failure at that level without invalidating the overall conclusion.\", \"Runtime estimates for Docta's diagnosis step are derived from the assumed small-MLP tabular encoder, not measured; the design treats per-step blowups as reportable findings.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-789583d4de4e4fa5a7157a350cbd844e", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver full rare-pattern Docta experiment design", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
